[h1]Galaxy Forge[/h1]

Galaxy Forge is a desktop editor for Stellaris galaxy maps. It runs outside the game and lets you edit both existing save files and galaxy scenarios.

You can move star systems, draw and cut hyperlanes, add new systems to an ongoing campaign, and inspect planets and their deposits. You can also turn a save into a scenario and start a new game from it with the Paint a Galaxy mod.

[b]Important:[/b] subscribing to this Workshop page does not install the editor. It contains no mod files; it exists so people can find Galaxy Forge here.

[b]Download:[/b] [url=https://github.com/IanHeinrich/stellaris-galaxy-forge/releases/latest]GitHub releases[/url]
Builds for Windows, macOS, and Linux. The Windows installer is signed, but because the certificate is new, SmartScreen may still show a warning. Click More info, then Run anyway. The macOS build is not signed.
[b]User guide:[/b] [url=https://github.com/IanHeinrich/stellaris-galaxy-forge/blob/main/docs/user-guide.md]how to use every part of the editor[/url]
[b]Source & bug tracker:[/b] [url=https://github.com/IanHeinrich/stellaris-galaxy-forge]GitHub[/url]

[h2]Editing a save[/h2]
Open a .sav file, make your changes, save it, and load it in Stellaris to continue the same campaign.
[list]
[*][b]Move systems:[/b] Drag a star system to a new location. Its hyperlanes move with it and their lengths are updated.
[*][b]Edit hyperlanes:[/b] Draw new lanes, cut existing ones, or disconnect a system completely. The Connect and Cut brushes let you work in broad strokes. Join reconnects separate parts of the galaxy.
[*][b]Add systems:[/b] Right-click empty space to add a new star system. Galaxy Forge generates it from your Stellaris installation's own rules, with its star, planets, moons, asteroid belts, and deposits. You can reroll it, choose its star, or rename it.
[*][b]Add special systems:[/b] The Special entry adds unique systems such as Zevox and special systems such as Trappist.
[*][b]Delete systems:[/b] Remove systems you added, one at a time or as a selection. The save's original systems cannot be deleted.
[*][b]Change stars:[/b] Change a star's type or size, or apply a star class to a selection of systems.
[*][b]Edit nebulae:[/b] Add, move, resize, and rename nebulae. Newly created nebulae use names from the game's own name list.
[*][b]Edit colours:[/b] On a Stellaris 4.5 save, change an empire's border and fill colours.
[*][b]Edit the L-Gate outcome:[/b] See which outcome the save rolled and change it until a gate opens. It stays hidden until you click Reveal.
[/list]

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/IanHeinrich/stellaris-galaxy-forge/main/workshop/images/save-editing.gif[/img]

[h2]Making a galaxy for a new game[/h2]
A scenario is a galaxy map that a new game starts from instead of generating a random galaxy.

Galaxy Forge can create a scenario from a blank map or from an existing save. When you open a save as a scenario, its layout, names, and fallen empires are preserved, while the original save remains unchanged.

Systems, hyperlanes, and nebulae work the same way as in a save. Scenarios also let you:
[list]
[*]Add systems by right-clicking.
[*]Choose what each system spawns in the initializer browser, which lists everything your installation and mods define and shows what each one places.
[*]Set spawn points and their weights, and mark hyperlanes that the game must never generate.
[*]Place fallen empire zones, marauder clans, and wormhole pairs.
[*]Set the counts offered on the new-game screen, such as the number of AI empires.
[/list]

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/IanHeinrich/stellaris-galaxy-forge/main/workshop/images/scenario-editing.gif[/img]

[h2]Painting & symmetry[/h2]
The Paint brush scatters clusters of systems and connects them with hyperlanes at the size and density you choose. Erase removes them again.

With symmetry on, every system, hyperlane, and brush stroke is mirrored around the galaxy centre or repeated 2–8 times around it.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/IanHeinrich/stellaris-galaxy-forge/main/workshop/images/paint-symmetry.gif[/img]

[h2]Playing your scenario[/h2]
To play your map you need the [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3532904115]Paint a Galaxy[/url] mod, plus the [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3762808682]Reserved Spawns[/url] submod if your map reserves seats.

Stellaris has some limitations with hand-made galaxies: empires can start on the wrong homeworlds, and marauders and fallen empires may fail to appear. Oatmeal Problem's Paint a Galaxy fixes these issues, and Galaxy Forge scenarios are built around it. Scenarios are written in the mod's format unless you disable that option.

When your map is ready:
[olist]
[*]Use File → Save into the Paint a Galaxy mod.
[*]Enable the mod in your Stellaris playset.
[*]Start a new game. Your custom map will appear as a galaxy size.
[/olist]
Steam may replace the mod's folder when the mod updates, so keep another copy of your map somewhere safe.

You do not need Paint a Galaxy to edit a save file.

[h2]What you can see[/h2]
Select a system to see its position, hyperlanes and their in-game lengths, bypasses, planets, fleets, flags, and initializer.

Every planet, moon, star, and asteroid has its own page with its deposits in the game's artwork, blockers and their clearing costs, and modifiers.

Press F to search for systems by name or by what they contain, such as "gaia" or "leviathan". Pin a search and its results stay highlighted on every save you open. The Layers menu shows and hides map layers.

In a scenario, the Scripts tab shows the initializer and the events and effects that affect a system, along with the file and line each comes from. This list is a best guess.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/IanHeinrich/stellaris-galaxy-forge/main/workshop/images/inspectors.png[/img]

[h2]Keeping your save safe[/h2]
Galaxy Forge edits save files at the byte level, so only the parts you change are rewritten. Everything else, including data from mods or newer game versions, is copied as it was.
[list]
[*]Each save keeps a timestamped backup of the previous file, up to eight per file.
[*]The first backup is the file you originally opened.
[*]If Stellaris saves over the file while it is open in Galaxy Forge, the editor asks before overwriting it.
[*]Every edit can be undone, and the Changes tab keeps track of them.
[*]The Issues tab checks for problems before you save, such as a hyperlane that exists at only one end.
[/list]

[h2]Mods[/h2]
Galaxy Forge reads game definitions, names, and artwork from your Stellaris installation and enabled mods. Nothing from the game is bundled.

Modded systems appear with their own names and artwork, and new systems are generated from the same files. Anything a mod puts in a save that Galaxy Forge does not understand is left untouched.

[h2]Limits[/h2]
Galaxy Forge is still in beta. Only the galaxy map can be edited. Empires, colonies, and fleets are shown but cannot be changed.

Galaxy Forge works with Stellaris 4.x saves, tested in-game on 4.4.6 and 4.5.0 with all DLC. Hyperlane editing also works on saves from 3.4 through 3.9. Adding systems requires a 4.x save and has been tested on 4.5.0.

Ironman saves are not supported. Adding systems is disabled for them, and the other edits have not been tested on Ironman.

Steam Cloud can restore the original save over your edited copy. Close Steam or disable Steam Cloud for Stellaris before playing an edited save.

Scenario editing is newer than save editing and has had less testing.

[h2]Bugs & requests[/h2]
If you find a bug or want to request something, open an issue on [url=https://github.com/IanHeinrich/stellaris-galaxy-forge/issues]GitHub[/url]. No GitHub account? Leave a comment on this page instead.

If you can, attach the save or scenario file. That makes the problem much easier to reproduce.
